meta {
  name: My projects
  type: http
  seq: 7
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/projects
  body: none
  auth: none
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 200
}

docs {
  Lists the projects visible to the caller, with the caller's role in each. Run
  it after sign-up or sign-in — the session cookie from the previous step is
  what authenticates this call.

  **Responses you will see**

  | Status | When |
  | --- | --- |
  | 200 with `[]` | Valid session, no visible project yet |
  | 200 with projects | Valid session, one or more visible projects |
  | 401 | No session cookie, or an expired session |

  **The role depends on how access arrived.** A caller with a project
  membership (`project_member`) gets that membership's effective role —
  `manager` or `assistant`. A caller with no project membership who is the
  organization's `admin` still sees the project and gets `admin`. Without an
  organization membership the project does not appear at all — that is data
  isolation between organizations.

  **Issue #40 (create project) does not exist yet.** Until then, the only way to
  have something to list is to seed a project by hand. After the sign-up in
  step 4:

      docker compose exec postgres psql -U bluprint -d bluprint

      -- the organization of whoever just signed up
      select o.id as organization_id, m.user_id
      from organization o
      join member m on m.organization_id = o.id
      where m.role = 'admin'
      order by m.created_at desc
      limit 1;

      -- create the project in that organization
      insert into project (organization_id, name)
      values ('<organization_id above>', 'Casa Moinhos');

  Run this request again: the project shows up with `role: "admin"`, because
  whoever created it through SQL got no `project_member` membership — exactly
  the gap #40 closes.
}
